  "opportunity_id": "proc_7164320154",
  "source": "content_cluster",
  "title": "Extending expired Sitara deals to cover unlinked meter flow",
  "task_class": "deal_entry_correction",
  "label_mechanical": "Deal entry and correction in Sitara / Unify / TAGG: extend flow / meter / deal",
  "narrative": {
    "what_happens": "During allocation, a scheduler finds measured gas flowing at a meter with no deal to link it to \u2014 usually because the Sitara purchase deal expired at month end, no new deal was referenced, or the evergreen flag was not set. The scheduler mails the deal owner or desk with the meter number, the deal number, the specific flow dates and volumes, and asks whether the deal can be extended or whether the volume should be allocated to \"strangers\". The recipient checks whether the extension is possible in Sitara/Unify and replies with either the extension, a rejection, or an alternative disposition such as letting the volume hit an OBA as a PPA. The exchange ends when the allocation exception clears; the resolution path is decided ad hoc in the thread rather than by a standing rule.",
    "where_it_stalls": "Nearly all messages in this group carry rework signals: the flow is discovered after the fact, so the fix is a retroactive amendment rather than correct entry. The escalation exemplars show the worst case \u2014 a request to extend a deal for flow dated June 1999 raised in November 2000, met with \"Is this date really June of 1999? Hopefully we're not just finding out about this?\" and \"I don't think we can even process this request\" because the desk was not on Unify at the time. When the extension is refused there is no defined fallback, so the thread turns into a negotiation over whether to allocate to strangers or route the volume to an OBA. Requests dominate over responses, so the requester is left waiting on a judgement call from another person.",
    "why_it_recurs": "Deals carry expiry dates and an evergreen flag while gas keeps physically flowing; monthly allocation is the first place the mismatch surfaces, so every unclosed valve or unrenewed deal reappears as an exception.",
    "automation_opportunity": "A scheduled check that compares meters with measured or nominated flow against deal expiry dates and evergreen flags in Sitara/Unify, and raises the gap before the allocation run rather than after \u2014 emitting one exception list per meter with the flow dates and volumes already filled in. Pair it with a written rule for the fallback disposition (extend, strangers, or OBA) so the decision is not renegotiated per thread.",
    "coherence": "tight"
  },
